      "text": "To find the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\), we first need to compute \\(11! + 12!\\). We can factor out \\(11!\\) from the expression:\n\n\\[11! + 12! = 11!(1 + 12) = 11! \\cdot 13\\]\n\nThis means that \\(11! + 12!\\) can be written as \\(11! \\cdot 13\\). To find the prime divisors of this expression, we need to find the prime divisors of \\(11!\\) and multiply them by 13.\n\nLet's find the prime divisors of \\(11!\\). The prime divisors of \\(11!\\) are:\n\n\\[2, 3, 5, 7, 11\\]\n\nNow, let's multiply these by 13 to find the positions of 13 in the factorization:\n\n\\[2 \\cdot 13 = 26\\]\n\\[3 \\cdot 13 = 39\\]\n\\[5 \\cdot 13 = 65\\]\n\\[7 \\cdot 13 = 91\\]\n\\[11 \\cdot 13 = 143\\]\n\nTherefore, the prime factors of \\(11! + 12!\\) are \\(13, 26, 39, 65, 91,\\) and \\(143\\). The largest prime factor among these is \\(143\\). \\(\\boxed{143}\\).",
