      "text": "To find the largest prime divisor of 11! + 12!, we can start by simplifying the expression.\n\n11! = 11 × 10 × 9 × ... × 1\n12! = 12 × 11 × 10 × ... × 1\n\nWe can factor out the 11! from both terms,\n\n11! + 12! = 11! (1 + 12)\n\nNow, 11! can be simplified further,\n\n11! = 11 × 10 × 9 × ... × 1\n\nHence, the expression becomes 11! (1 + 12) = 11! × 13 which is\n\n11! = 79833600 and 13 = 19\n\nA prime number is a number greater than 1 that cannot be expressed as the product of two smaller integers. In this case, 13 is a prime number and divides 13 exactly 10 times.\n\nHowever, 13 is not a divisor of 11!, as it is not a divisor of any of the odd factors less than 11.\n\nThe largest prime divisor of 11! + 12! is therefore 13, indicating that 13 is the largest prime that divides 11! + 12! exactly. The answer is 13.",
