      "text": "Let \\( x = \\text{lcm}(11!, 12!) \\). Then \\( x \\) is a divisor of both \\( 11! + 12! \\) and \\( 12! \\). Note that \\( 11! = 1 \\times 2 \\times \\cdots \\times 11 \\).\n\nCalculations:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{cccccc}\n\\text{Case} & k & C(11,k) & k & D \\\\\n\\hline\n0 & 11 & 1 & 11 & A\\\\\n1 & 11 & 11 & 11 & B\\\\\n2 & 10 & 55 & 55 & C\\\\\n3 & 9 & 165 & 165 & A\\\\\n4 & 8 & 220 & 220 & B\\\\\n5 & 7 & 2310 & 2310 & C\\\\\n6 & 6 & 2730 & 2730 & A\\\\\n7 & 5 & 33010 & 33010 & B\\\\\n8 & 4 & 39900 & 39900 & C\\\\\n9 & 3 & 46200 & 46200 & A\\\\\n10 & 2 & 50050 & 50050 & B\\\\\n11 & 1 & 555866 \\text{ }| \\text{ } 555866 & 555866 & C \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( x = \\text{lcm} (11!, 12!) = 33010 \\). Therefore, the largest prime divisor of \\( 11! + 12! \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).\n\nPlease share similar solutions: to improve mathematical endurance, students should increase the number of problems they solve, focusing on reasonable and easy problems and avoiding complex ones; adopt effective learning strategies, focusing on reviewing the smartphone, notes, and old papers after learning Math Xi.\n\nWhen presented with a question like this, the solution to first find the greatest common divisor of \\(11! \\) and \\(12!\\) and then find the prime numbers between the answer and \\(12! \\), which is the answer's greatest common divisor is \\(10\\) \\(\\times \\larger{lcm}\\) greater than the answer. This problem requires deep understanding of when to use the greatest common divisor and when to use the greatest common divisor's answer's multiple.",
